<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1351">1351</a>–<a href="/years/1402">1402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gian Galeazzo Visconti ruled part of the Milanese lands from 1378, seized sole power from his uncle Bernabò in 1385 and in 1395 bought from King Wenceslaus the title of Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He conquered Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, Siena, Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and seemed close to dominating northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> when he died of plague in 1402. He began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> and the Certosa of Pavia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti.jpg" alt="Gian Galeazzo Visconti" width="310" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Giovanni Ambrogio de Predis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GiovAmbrogiodePredisattribGianGaleazzoVisconti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
